    "unresolvedReleaseQuestionCount": 5,
    "unresolvedReleaseQuestions": [
      {
        "trackId": "wv_drug_conditional_discharge",
        "affectedElement": "correct_form",
        "question": "Whether a West Virginia Judiciary form exists for the § 60A-4-407 conditional discharge expungement application. The Court Forms index was read at source on 2026-08-06 and publishes exactly five expungement forms — SCA-C900, SCA-C903, SCA-C906, SCA-C907 and SCA-C912 — none of which is a § 60A-4-407 application. On that basis the review's build blocker is answered in the negative and the route is a custom pleading. Whether an unpublished local form is used in some circuits was not tested."
      },
      {
        "trackId": "wv_drug_conditional_discharge",
        "affectedElement": "eligibility_branch",
        "question": "Whether relief under § 60A-4-407 counts against the once-per-lifetime limit in § 61-11-26(o). Section 61-11-26(o) reaches only §§ 61-11-26 and 61-11-26a on its face, and § 60A-4-407 was read in full at source on 2026-08-06 and contains no cross-reference to it. The textual reading is that these are separate limits in separate statutes, but the consequence of getting it wrong is permanent."
      },
      {
        "trackId": "wv_pardon_expungement",
        "affectedElement": "filing_process",
        "question": "What a Class I legal advertisement costs and how the time and place of the hearing are fixed in practice, since the notice must state them and must be published before the petition is made. Article 3 of chapter 59 governs the form and the qualified publication, but the sequencing between obtaining a hearing date from the clerk and running the advertisement is a matter of local practice that the controlling review did not establish and that decides both the pricing message and the participant instructions."
      },
      {
        "trackId": "wv_pardon_expungement",
        "affectedElement": "legal_effect_or_warning",
        "question": "Whether § 5-1-16a(b) is the whole of the effect of a pardon expungement. The subsection was read at source on 2026-08-06 and provides only that the expunged record may not be considered in an application to a West Virginia educational institution or in an application for a licence required by a West Virginia professional organisation. Unlike §§ 61-11-25(e) and 61-11-26(l), it contains no general non-disclosure provision and no statement that the proceedings are considered never to have occurred. What the packet may tell a participant about disclosing the record to an ordinary employer after a § 5-1-16a expungement is therefore not settled."
      },
      {
        "trackId": "wv_pardon_expungement",
        "affectedElement": "correct_form",
        "question": "Whether a West Virginia Judiciary form exists for the § 5-1-16a pardon expungement petition. The Court Forms index was read at source on 2026-08-06 and publishes exactly five expungement forms — SCA-C900, SCA-C903, SCA-C906, SCA-C907 and SCA-C912 — none of which is a § 5-1-16a petition. On that basis the review's build blocker is answered in the negative and the route is a custom pleading. Whether an unpublished local form is used in some circuits was not tested."
      }
    ],
